"Cursive script was made for speed. The same dictionary says it appeared so that people could write conveniently and quickly. Strokes are joined, shortened, or left out, so a whole character can become one or two flowing lines. It is the fastest of the three, and the hardest to read.": "6971d461d305",
"Did cursive script come from writing regular script quickly? What does the lesson say?": "366c02358ab6",
"Here is a surprise: cursive script is not regular script written in a hurry. It began around the Qin and Han dynasties as a quick way to write clerical script. Regular script took shape later, in the Later Han dynasty, and was complete by the Wei. So the three scripts are three ways of writing, and all three are still used today.": "2aaeb60e44d7",
"I did not recognize my cousin in his new glasses.": "b7812ddd8dac",
"In regular script, every stroke is written separately. The brush starts each stroke with care, finishes it with care, and then leaves the paper before the next one. This makes regular script the clearest of the three, and the slowest. It is the form you see in textbooks and learn first.": "f2867f67531d",
"In this lesson, three brushes write the same character at the same time, and you can see which one finishes first. Then name all five scripts you have met in this unit, and use the stopwatch on the practice pad to find your own three speeds.": "507173fe42d8",
"Join the two dots with a straight line.": "73763eb43ef1",
